mh2lm: preflight PMIC IRQ routes for hardware keys (0128)

The first `SweetSpmiInterruptMux` consumer will be the already-working power,
volume-up and volume-down key path. Before it can claim the PMIC-arb's shared
GIC SPI 481, MH2LM needs an on-device record of the PON and PM8150 GPIO6
routes and the PMIC peripheral's raw interrupt configuration. The existing
battery preflight establishes this approach for PM8150B charger sources, but
it does not inspect the PON/GPIO APIDs that the key driver actually uses.

Add an opt-in `sweetkeysirqdiag=1` preflight. It initializes the existing
read-only SPMI observer helper if necessary, then logs each key peripheral's
APID mapping, owner, PMIC-arb IRQ-owner bit, owner bitmap, channel access
enable and channel IRQ status. The key driver separately logs raw RT_STS,
SET_TYPE, POLARITY_HIGH, POLARITY_LOW, EN_SET and LATCHED_CLR bytes for PON
and GPIO6. Raw whole-byte output avoids guessing the exact key IRQ bit before
the target hardware confirms it.

This patch does not register a GIC callback, change PDC configuration, enable
or clear any PMIC interrupt, or change the 20 ms key-polling fallback. It adds
a generic read-only per-source route inspection API that the later static
SweetSpmiInterruptMux route table can reuse.

--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c
@@ -120,6 +120,26 @@
 	IOLockUnlock(sp_lock);
 }
 
+void
+sweet_spmi_irq_diag_source(unsigned sid, unsigned peripheral, const char *name)
+{
+	if (!sp_ready) {
+		printf("SWEET SPMI IRQ: %s skipped; arbiter is not ready\n",
+		    name ? name : "source");
+		return;
+	}
+	if (sid > 15u || peripheral > 0xffu) {
+		printf("SWEET SPMI IRQ: %s invalid sid=%u peripheral=%#x\n",
+		    name ? name : "source", sid, peripheral);
+		return;
+	}
+	IOLockLock(sp_lock);
+	printf("SWEET SPMI IRQ: %s read-only source preflight; no GIC handler, no enable, no clear\n",
+	    name ? name : "source");
+	sp_irq_diag_peripheral(sid, peripheral, name ? name : "source");
+	IOLockUnlock(sp_lock);
+}
